s/- `P = Sym_Z\(V\)`, a polynomial-type domain on the symbols `t_v`, `v ∈ V`, with\n  `t_\(v\+w\) = t_v \+ t_w`\./- `P = Sym_Z(V)`, a domain generated by symbols `t_v`, `v ∈ V`, with\n  `t_(v+w) = t_v + t_w`. Write `t = t_v` for `v = 1`, and `t_k = t_(s^k)`./;
s/presented, as a ring, by the generators `t_0 = t_1̂`, `x\^\(±1\)`, `y\^\(±1\)` and the\n   relations/presented, as a ring, by the generators `t`, `x^(±1)`, `y^(±1)` and the\n   relations/;
s/   - `y t_0 y\^-1 = t_0 \+ x t_0 x\^-1`;\n   - `t_0 · x t_0 x\^-1 = x t_0 x\^-1 · t_0`\.\n\n   Here `t_1̂` denotes `t_v` for `v = 1 ∈ V`\. The second/   - `y t y^-1 = t + x t x^-1`;\n   - `t · x t x^-1 = x t x^-1 · t`.\n\n   The second/;
s/forces all\n   conjugates of `t_0` to commute/forces all\n   conjugates of `t` to commute/;
s/`\[t_0, x\^k t_0 x\^-k\] = 0` for all `k`\. Adding the letter `y` with\n  `y t_0 y\^-1 = t_0 \+ t_1`/`[t, x^k t x^-k] = 0` for all `k`. Adding the letter `y` with\n  `y t y^-1 = t + t_1`/;
